<gpx version="1.1" creator="Cotswold Way planner" xmlns="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1 http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1/gpx.xsd">
<metadata><name>Cotswold Way — 2 days, camping</name></metadata>
<wpt lat="51.540303" lon="-2.413362"><ele>0.0</ele><name>ARRIVE — Yate station (YAE)</name><desc>Y8 bus to Wotton, 45–50 min, Mon–Sat only</desc><sym>Car</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.377602" lon="-2.356719"><ele>0.0</ele><name>Bath Spa station (BTH)</name><desc>Finish. 0.3 mi from the Abbey.</desc><sym>Car</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.638443" lon="-2.349425"><ele>79.5</ele><name>START — Wotton-under-Edge</name><desc>31.5 mi to Bath</desc><sym>Lodging</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.508025" lon="-2.339228"><ele>170.8</ele><name>NIGHT 1 — Tormarton</name><desc>17.1 mi to Bath</desc><sym>Lodging</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.381368" lon="-2.359475"><ele>31.5</ele><name>FINISH — Bath Abbey</name><desc>0.0 mi to Bath</desc><sym>Lodging</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.581503" lon="-2.327815"><ele>196.7</ele><name>CAMP: France Lane Farm</name><desc>800 m off the path, 24.0 mi to Bath. ~£12</desc><sym>Campground</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.561351" lon="-2.340911"><ele>164.3</ele><name>CAMP: Oakfield Farm</name><desc>1.6 km off the path, 22.2 mi to Bath. </desc><sym>Campground</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.556147" lon="-2.345350"><ele>149.3</ele><name>CAMP: Starvale / Hideaway Horton</name><desc>~500 m off the path, 21.5 mi to Bath. </desc><sym>Campground</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.508025" lon="-2.339228"><ele>170.8</ele><name>CAMP: Compass Inn (informal tents)</name><desc>1 km off the path, 17.1 mi to Bath. free / donation</desc><sym>Campground</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.429850" lon="-2.393226"><ele>199.9</ele><name>CAMP: Lansdown Campsite</name><desc>on route off the path, 7.9 mi to Bath. </desc><sym>Campground</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.397035" lon="-2.404113"><ele>116.3</ele><name>CAMP: Cleeve Hill</name><desc>430 m off the path, 3.1 mi to Bath. </desc><sym>Campground</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.394948" lon="-2.400174"><ele>100.8</ele><name>CAMP: Penn Hill CL</name><desc>240 m off the path, 2.9 mi to Bath. </desc><sym>Campground</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.622730" lon="-2.339490"><ele>0.0</ele><name>DIVERSION: Emergency closure near Wortley</name><desc>Emergency closure order after drought and high winds. National Trails say a diversion will be in place and that further details are to follow; nothing had been published when this page was built. The band shown is approximate - the actual e</desc><sym>Danger Area</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.493710" lon="-2.347010"><ele>0.0</ele><name>DIVERSION: Ongoing diversion, Beacon Lane Plantation</name><desc>Listed as an ongoing diversion near Tormarton. Follow the waymarking on the ground.</desc><sym>Danger Area</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.638320" lon="-2.348300"><ele>0.0</ele><name>The Ancient Ram Inn</name><desc>31.5 mi to Bath. A former inn dating to about 1145 and, by reputation, one of the most haunted buildings in England. Now a private house; you pass the door.</desc><sym>Museum</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.638830" lon="-2.322700"><ele>0.0</ele><name>Newark Park</name><desc>30.0 mi to Bath. A Tudor hunting lodge perched on the edge of the escarpment, later remodelled as a country house. National Trust, and a worthwhile detour on a short day.</desc><sym>Museum</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.638290" lon="-2.326040"><ele>0.0</ele><name>Blackquarries Hill Long Barrow</name><desc>29.9 mi to Bath. Another Neolithic barrow, right at the top of the long climb out of Wotton.</desc><sym>Museum</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.587240" lon="-2.329440"><ele>0.0</ele><name>Somerset Monument</name><desc>24.5 mi to Bath. A 120 ft tower of 1846 to Lord Robert Somerset, who commanded cavalry at Waterloo. Closed to climb, but it is the landmark of this stretch.</desc><sym>Museum</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.562260" lon="-2.335600"><ele>0.0</ele><name>Horton Court</name><desc>22.5 mi to Bath. A Norman hall of about 1140 attached to a Tudor house - one of the oldest continuously inhabited buildings in England. National Trust, limited opening.</desc><sym>Museum</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.558510" lon="-2.341680"><ele>0.0</ele><name>Horton Camp</name><desc>22.0 mi to Bath. Iron Age hillfort on the edge, with the Severn Vale laid out below.</desc><sym>Museum</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.543850" lon="-2.346980"><ele>0.0</ele><name>Little Sodbury Manor</name><desc>20.4 mi to Bath. William Tyndale was tutor here around 1522, and it was here he argued that the Bible should be in English - the idea the monument back at North Nibley commemorates.</desc><sym>Museum</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.541810" lon="-2.346960"><ele>0.0</ele><name>Old Sodbury Camp</name><desc>20.2 mi to Bath. Iron Age hillfort with unusually clear double ramparts. The climb up to it is only 159 ft.</desc><sym>Museum</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.486200" lon="-2.370710"><ele>0.0</ele><name>Hinton Hill</name><desc>13.4 mi to Bath. Traditionally identified with the Battle of Deorham of 577, where the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle records the West Saxons taking Gloucester, Cirencester and Bath. The identification is old and not certain.</desc><sym>Museum</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.479900" lon="-2.375810"><ele>0.0</ele><name>Dyrham Park</name><desc>12.8 mi to Bath. A late 17th-century mansion in a deer park, built on Virginia tobacco money. The Cotswold Way runs round the park wall; entry is 23-25 pounds and the tea room is for members.</desc><sym>Museum</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.431130" lon="-2.397910"><ele>0.0</ele><name>Lansdown battlefield</name><desc>7.6 mi to Bath. The Battle of Lansdown, 5 July 1643. Royalist Cornish pike drove Waller&#x27;s Parliamentarians off the crest, at a cost that made it a hollow victory.</desc><sym>Museum</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.431450" lon="-2.401240"><ele>0.0</ele><name>Sir Bevil Grenville&#x27;s Monument</name><desc>7.4 mi to Bath. Erected 1720 where Grenville fell leading the Cornish charge. It stands right beside the path on the ridge.</desc><sym>Museum</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.424970" lon="-2.407290"><ele>0.0</ele><name>Roman villa remains</name><desc>6.2 mi to Bath. Foundations of a Roman villa on the slope below the ridge - a reminder that Bath was a Roman town and this was its hinterland.</desc><sym>Museum</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.413040" lon="-2.414010"><ele>0.0</ele><name>Prospect Stile</name><desc>4.6 mi to Bath. The moment Bath appears: the whole city in its bowl, with Solsbury Hill beyond. A kissing gate now rather than a stile.</desc><sym>Museum</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.405570" lon="-2.415940"><ele>0.0</ele><name>Kelston Round Hill</name><desc>3.9 mi to Bath. The tree clump you can see from half of Somerset, 218 m, a two-minute detour off the path.</desc><sym>Museum</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.386120" lon="-2.370350"><ele>0.0</ele><name>Royal Crescent</name><desc>0.8 mi to Bath. Thirty houses behind one continuous Ionic facade, built 1767-74 by John Wood the Younger. The trail walks the lawn in front of it.</desc><sym>Museum</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.385650" lon="-2.363940"><ele>0.0</ele><name>Bath Assembly Rooms</name><desc>0.4 mi to Bath. Where Georgian Bath danced, gambled and matched its daughters. Gutted in the 1942 Blitz and rebuilt.</desc><sym>Museum</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.381310" lon="-2.359860"><ele>0.0</ele><name>Roman Baths</name><desc>0.0 mi to Bath. The hot spring the Romans built Aquae Sulis around, and the temple of Sulis Minerva beside it. Fifty metres from the finish.</desc><sym>Museum</sym></wpt>
<wpt lat="51.381370" lon="-2.359470"><ele>0.0</ele><name>Bath Abbey</name><desc>0.0 mi to Bath. The end of the trail. There is a Cotswold Way marker set into the pavement on the Abbey churchyard.</desc><sym>Museum</sym></wpt>
<trk><name>Cotswold Way Day 1 — Wotton-under-Edge to Tormarton</name><desc>14.4 mi, +494 m / -403 m. 7 hrs</desc><trkseg>
